                <pb facs="0778" n="680" sameAs="frus:frus1920v01/pg680"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">861.77/1363: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The President of the Technical Board</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Stevens</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Harbin</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1920-02-11T22:00:00+08:26"><hi rendition="#italic">February 11,
                                1920—10 p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received February 21—5
                        a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>Kolchak, Pepeliaev shot at Irkutsk February 7. General Horvat advises us Japanese
                    Government through the bank offers railway <pb facs="0779" n="681"
                        xml:id="pg681"/> five year loan of 20,000,000 yen without security, simply
                    receipt of railway; asks my advice, told him to let it alone. If consummated it
                    means Japanese domination and I cannot conceive any policy that could be
                    defended that would permit of this. Chinese Government trying to appoint the
                    majority directors [of] Chinese Eastern, seeking for control of railway.
                    Covertly threatening to take charge by force, but I believe are helpless alone.
                    Culmination Chinese Eastern affairs appears to be imminent and if they eventuate
                    as now seems probable, the United States will have much to regret in the
                    future.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Stevens</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
